"New insights from Google’s AI & Economy ATLAS Google has announced new interactive data and research findings from the 'AI & Economy ATLAS', analyzing global AI adoption across occupations and how scientists utilize AI. According to joint research by Google, Google DeepMind, and MIT FutureTech, nearly half of surveyed scientists use AI daily, saving approximately 7 hours per week. AI adoption patterns vary by country and occupation: creative fields such as arts and design stood out in India (19%), while technical roles such as computer and mathematics predominated in the US (30%). While computer and mathematics, as well as business and finance, lead AI usage in OECD countries, office administration, arts and media, and education ranked highest in non-OECD countries. Mistral raises €3B to make sovereign, open-weight AI the technology frontier French AI startup Mistral secures a €3B Series D funding round at a valuation exceeding €21B, aiming to expand sovereign open-weight AI research and infrastructure. Mistral raised a €3B Series D round led by Samsung Electronics, marking the largest equity funding round in European tech history The raised capital will be allocated toward scaling frontier research, expanding compute capacity to train powerful models, and accelerating global commercialization and infrastructure deployment Reinforces a full-stack open-weight sovereign AI approach that guarantees control over data, models, computing, and production systems Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Cloudera and Mistral AI have partnered to empower enterprises to build and run customized AI models within their own environments while maintaining data sovereignty. Integrates Mistral's models with Cloudera's hybrid data platform to enable direct AI inference execution across on-premises, public/private cloud, and fully air-gapped environments. Enables enterprises in regulated industries (such as finance, manufacturing, and telecommunications) to train and build customized AI models using decades of proprietary data while retaining data ownership. Supports a shift from renting generic AI to owning tailored AI, responding to the demand for Sovereign AI that keeps data, intelligence, and compute infrastructure under customer control. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. A case study on successfully migrating 40,000 lines of legacy Fortran 77 code to modern C++ using AI agents and a numerical verification framework. Migrated a 40,000-line physics-intensive reservoir simulator (Fortran 77) lacking test suites or documentation into modern C++. Moved beyond simple syntax translation by applying a systematic workflow that combined building a parity harness for numerical verification, prior documentation via AI agents, and human review. Resolved Fortran 77-specific global memory (COMMON blocks) and implicit typing constraints, integrating with object-oriented C++ and modern scientific computing frameworks like PETSc. Mistral x HUMAIN Mistral and Saudi Arabia's HUMAIN have entered into a strategic partnership worth hundreds of millions of euros to build sovereign AI infrastructure in the Middle East and develop frontier models specialized in Arabic. Collaboration on supporting sovereign AI infrastructure, model localization, and deploying advanced solutions including cybersecurity and voice for Saudi Arabia and the Middle East region Development of high-performance Arabic-capable frontier models and addressing local compute demand by leveraging HUMAIN's data center infrastructure Pursuing a joint go-to-market strategy that provides customized open-weight models and ensures data sovereignty for regulated industries (finance, manufacturing, telecommunications, public sector, etc.) Agentic Search. More accurate and efficient results from your AI systems. Mistral AI has announced Agentic Search, a multi-step retrieval layer that improves accuracy while reducing latency and token usage across complex internal organizational documents. It provides a multi-step retrieval loop to explore and verify long, dense documents based on existing search indexes using five tools: search, open, navigate, read, and grep. It supports on-premises and cloud-isolated environments, enabling deployment without crossing the security boundaries of sensitive domain-specific data. It significantly improves accuracy on finance and office document benchmarks while drastically reducing p90 latency and token consumption. Heart of the Matter: How a Major Children’s Hospital Uses Open Source NVIDIA AI for Cardiac Care Children's Hospital of Philadelphia has built a system utilizing NVIDIA's open-source healthcare AI framework to generate precision 3D heart models for pediatric patients with congenital heart disease in just seconds. Children's Hospital of Philadelphia (CHOP) reduced pediatric cardiac 3D modeling time from four hours to just seconds using MONAI, an open-source framework co-developed by NVIDIA. Using MONAI Label and NVIDIA Auto3DSeg, precision segmentation models are trained on CT, MRI, and 3D echocardiogram images to support customized preoperative treatment planning. More than 20 children's hospitals across the U.S. have adopted similar programs, and CHOP aims for approximately 200 cases this year while expanding beyond cardiology into other medical specialties. Former TikTok execs built an app that uses AI to teach you how to pose for a photo Former TikTok executives have launched 'Superpose,' an iOS camera app that uses AI to recommend and guide users through flattering poses when taking photos. When taking a selfie or portrait, the AI generates four poses and guides the user to match the pose within the frame. The service focuses on coaching real-life portrait photography rather than virtual backgrounds or synthetic image generation. The company raised $2.2 million in funding from Khosla Ventures, Meitu, and others. Discover how to take your startup from prototype to production at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 An introduction to a session at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 on how to scale a startup from prototype to mass production, along with registration details Sharing startup scale-up know-how on transitioning prototypes to production at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 Providing insights from industry leaders including Adrian Macneil (Foxglove), John Mackey (MBRYONICS), and Boris Sofman (Bedrock Robotics) Offering discounts of up to $200 on pass purchases when registering before September 25 Early Anthropic hire, former METR COO have found a way to rein in rogue AI agents AIUC, a startup founded by an early Anthropic employee and a former METR COO, has raised $40 million for its service verifying and certifying malfunctions and security risks in enterprise AI agents. Early Anthropic alumnus Rune Kvist and former METR COO Rajiv Dattani co-founded AIUC, a startup that audits and certifies AI agents against jailbreaks, hallucinations, and data leaks. Modeled after the SOC 2 cybersecurity standard, the company developed the 'AIUC-1' standard incorporating feedback from roughly 250 security and risk leaders, alongside a testing suite of about 5,000 tests. The company raised a $40 million Series A led by Ribbit Capital, bringing total cumulative funding to $55 million, and secured clients including Cursor, Lovable, Harvey, and ElevenLabs. Salesforce and Nvidia’s new reasoning model is everything the AI labs should fear Salesforce has partnered with Nvidia to unveil Koa, an enterprise-tailored reasoning AI model based on open-weight Nemotron. Salesforce announced Koa, its first reasoning model specialized for sales, marketing, and customer support, built on Nvidia's Nemotron open-weight model. Post-training was conducted using virtually simulated synthetic data instead of real customer data, eliminating the risk of data leaks and enhancing security. Koa is offered on the Agentforce platform as an alternative to existing frontier closed models (such as Claude and ChatGPT), helping enterprises reduce token consumption and AI costs. ZGCM-1: A Fully Open and Extremely Efficient Foundation Model for Math and Agentic Search ZGCM-1, a highly efficient open-source foundation model that combines deep internal reasoning with external tool usage to deliver frontier-scale performance in mathematics and agentic search, has been released. Efficiently supports a 256K context window through architectural and system co-design, combining gated sliding-window and full attention alongside a stable FP8 Muon optimizer. Introduces a progressive curriculum expanding across 16K, 64K, and 256K, mid-training that reconstructs interaction trajectories into Markov Decision Processes (MDP), and an agent swarm-based R&D workflow. Despite having only 7B parameters, it demonstrates performance on par with substantially larger frontier models such as Qwen3-235B-A22B and GLM-5.1 across mathematical reasoning and agentic search benchmarks, with all weights and training code fully open-sourced. Converge Then Diversify: Decoupling Convergence and Diversity in Multi-Objective Bayesian Optimisation A study proposing the 'Converge Then Diversify (CTD)' technique, which decouples convergence and diversity in multi-objective Bayesian optimization to explore sequentially. Existing multi-objective Bayesian optimization (MOBO) approaches simultaneously pursue convergence toward the Pareto front and diversity, which creates limitations in adequately approaching the entire front when evaluation budgets are severely constrained. This paper introduces a two-stage CTD (converge-then-diversify) scheme that decouples convergence and diversity, rapidly converging to a single point on the Pareto front in the first stage and diversifying solutions across the entire front in the second stage. Experimental results show that across 446 pairwise comparisons, CTD demonstrated statistically superior performance compared to existing state-of-the-art methods in 72.9% of cases, proving outstanding efficiency particularly under severely limited search budgets or in high-dimensional problems. Generalized Agent Iteration: One Formal Framework for Iterative Policy Improvement and Recursive Self-Improvement This paper proposes Generalized Agent Iteration (GAI), a framework that unifies iterative policy improvement and recursive self-improvement (RSI) into a single learning paradigm to enable comparison and analysis. It formalizes recursive self-improvement (RSI) and classical generalized policy iteration (GPI) into a single unified learning framework called 'Generalized Agent Iteration (GAI)'. It categorizes systems and enables comparisons among existing instances along two core axes: whether the improvement mechanism resides internally or externally to the agent, and whether evaluation criteria are grounded externally. It classifies system polarity into anchored, goal drift, and fully self-referential states based on whether evaluation criteria are grounded, providing a theoretical foundation to formalize failure modes of RSI under various conditions. Vibe Patenting: Evaluating LLM Judges for Professional Patent-Drafting Agents A study analyzing the utility and limitations of LLM judges that evaluate drafts from professional patent-drafting AI agents and provide iterative improvement feedback. Built an end-to-end patent drafting testbed named Vibe Patenting to verify the effectiveness of evaluation and feedback from LLM judges Through iterative revisions based on judge feedback, even low-cost agents with lower reasoning capabilities can achieve quality approaching that of much more expensive, high-performance reasoning agents Comparisons with independent evaluations by professional patent attorneys showed significant agreement, though metric-specific discrepancies and systematic calibration errors were also identified Toward Self-Adaptive Physical AI: Can LLM Agents Manage Long-Horizon Physical Tasks? This study explores the potential of LLM-based physical AI agents to autonomously perform long-horizon physical tasks while adapting to environmental changes without human intervention. A multi-agent framework integrating planning, tool calling, observation, and verification was designed to evaluate the capability of performing long-horizon physical tasks. A comparative evaluation against reinforcement learning (RL) agents was conducted on agricultural tasks under various weather conditions. The zero-shot LLM agent demonstrated performance comparable to the reinforcement learning agent under the same weather conditions and exhibited superior adaptability over RL when environmental changes occurred. BudgetBench: A Budget-Tiered Protocol and Pilot Harness for Memory Strategy Evaluation in Local Large Language Model Agents Introduces BudgetBench, a benchmark protocol and harness for quantitatively evaluating diverse memory strategies in local large language model agent environments under input token budget constraints. Sets the per-call input token budget as an independent variable, scaling it across 2K, 4K, 8K, 16K, and 32K tokens while measuring quality, budget utilization, latency, and budget violation rates. Open-sources a reusable measurement harness featuring swappable memory strategy interfaces, strict budget enforcement, and prompt audit metadata. Identifies budget compliance failures, non-monotonic quality curves, and operational limits through a pilot study that were not visible in single-budget evaluations. A derivative-fidelity failure mode in physics-informed neural networks: strengthened benchmark evidence from function-value training A study demonstrating through 1D benchmark experiments that even when function value approximation is accurate in physics-informed neural networks (PINNs), significant derivative errors can occur when obtained via automatic differentiation. Identified a 'derivative-fidelity' failure mode where high agreement in function values does not guarantee the accuracy of higher-order derivatives computed via automatic differentiation Trained multilayer perceptrons solely on function values of sin(x) and exp(x) and separately evaluated second-order derivatives, confirming large second-order derivative errors even with visually accurate function approximations Proposed a diagnostic protocol distinguishing function value accuracy from physical residual reliability through training data density, activation functions, boundary-focused evaluation, and L2 and maximum error diagnostics LLMs or Naive Bayes? Old Gems or New Ways A research finding showing that when labeled data is available for text classification tasks, the traditional Naive Bayes algorithm delivers accuracy comparable to large language models (LLMs) alongside overwhelming processing speed and energy efficiency. While LLMs have an advantage in zero-shot settings with no data, Naive Bayes outperforms zero-shot LLMs on tasks with low likelihood of data contamination. For the AG News task with labeled data, Naive Bayes achieved 89.1% accuracy, rivaling or exceeding 27B and 397B LLMs, while processing thousands of samples per second even on standard CPU environments. GPU batch inference for small LLMs is 40 to 486 times slower than CPU inference for Naive Bayes, and a Kubernetes Helm operator is also provided to automate model selection in resource-constrained environments. Evaluating LLM-Generated Rules for Heart Disease Prediction A study comparing and analyzing the performance and interpretability of traditional machine learning models and large language model (LLM)-generated rule-based systems in predicting heart disease. Traditional machine learning models consistently outperformed LLM-generated rule-based systems in predictive performance. Random Forest achieved the highest predictive performance among all models, with an accuracy of 90.2% and an F1-score of 0.906. Although LLM-based rule models showed comparatively lower predictive performance, they provide interpretable IF-THEN diagnostic logic that enhances explainability and transparency in clinical decision-making. Machine Unlearning for Speech Question Answering in Large Audio-Language Models This study investigates machine unlearning techniques to effectively eliminate the memorization of sensitive personal data during speech question answering in Large Audio-Language Models (LALMs). It addresses the challenges of machine unlearning in LALM environments, which are more demanding than traditional text-based LLMs due to the combined nature of acoustic perception and factual knowledge. The study proposes and evaluates various unlearning techniques, including Gradient Ascent, Task Arithmetic, and safe-refusal alignment fine-tuning, to remove sensitive knowledge while preserving general performance. Experimental results on speech question answering datasets demonstrate a reduction in the privacy leakage rate by up to 80% while maintaining general speech comprehension capabilities. Token Merging for Multilingual Speech Recognition: A Systematic Study Across Model Scale and Fine-Tuning This study explores applying token merging techniques to Whisper, a multilingual speech recognition model, to improve computational efficiency and reduce deployment costs without sacrificing accuracy. Systematically evaluated token merging techniques across the Whisper model family, dynamically combining redundant features during inference without retraining to reduce sequence length. Validated the effectiveness of token merging across 16 languages, 3 model sizes, and under DoRA fine-tuning setups for low-resource languages. Confirmed that computational efficiency improved significantly with negligible transcription accuracy loss across most low-resource languages and model sizes, continuing to function effectively even after fine-tuning. PhysMent: An Interactive Approach For LLM Reasoning In Physics Problems This study introduces PhysMent, a benchmark designed to evaluate the physical reasoning and experimentation capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) through active, iterative interaction with a physics simulator. Unlike static benchmarks that provide all numerical values upfront, it requires models to actively explore information by directly experimenting with applying forces, querying states, advancing time, and modifying geometry via the MuJoCo physics simulator. Comprising 105 classical mechanics scenes across 4 difficulty levels, 3 scene modalities, and a 6-dimensional evaluation framework, it comprehensively measures the multi-step experimentation abilities of models. While models achieved up to 80% accuracy on qualitative, single-concept tasks, performance dropped sharply below 30% for most models on high-difficulty tasks requiring precise multi-step experimental procedures, highlighting procedural tool use as a major bottleneck. Lexical Prompt Compression for Large Language Models: A Training-Free, Deterministic Pipeline with Empirical Pareto Analysis Across Eleven Task Categories This study analyzes a pipeline and its performance for deterministically compressing prompts for large language models using only classical lexical natural language processing techniques without additional model training. Proposed a CPU-based training-free prompt compression pipeline combining 11 transformation techniques, including stop-word removal, fluff phrase elimination, and lemmatization. Evaluated the compression ratio and output quality preservation across 15 configurations using 1,242 prompts from 6 datasets and 18,630 GPT-4o-mini generation outputs. Achieved an average token reduction of 40.3% and a BERTScore-F1 of 0.876 under the most aggressive compression setting, but identified limitations showing pronounced performance degradation under excessive compression in common-sense reasoning tasks. TestHallVQA: Exploring LVLMs' Document-Level Reasoning under Redundant Contexts from Scientific Exams Introduces TestHallVQA, a new benchmark that evaluates the document-level reasoning capabilities of Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) within redundant and irrelevant visual contexts based on scientific exam problems. Existing Visual Question Answering (VQA) benchmarks have been biased toward simple document understanding or single-page reasoning, limiting their ability to quantify performance degradation caused by irrelevant visual tokens. Proposed TestHallVQA, a multi-image benchmark combining human-exam-level difficulty with document-scale context, capable of controlled injection of multi-level context redundancy. Introduced F1-R², a new metric evaluating both computational reasoning competence and evidence retrieval robustness under redundant environments, and identified the limitations of major LVLM models. Clinical Reasoning Under a Partially Observed Objective in Cone Beam CT Report Generation This study analyzes the limitations of lexical overlap-centric evaluation metrics in cone beam CT (CBCT) maxillofacial report generation and proposes a clinical reasoning model that directly optimizes factual entailment. In a composite evaluation metric consisting of lexical overlap (20%) and LLM-based factual entailment (80%), the authors found that pursuing simple n-gram overlap alone significantly degrades entailment precision. The study revealed that sentence selection in reports could be predicted with high accuracy using only image header information and scanning institution data, demonstrating that existing lexical metrics reward dictation conventions rather than anatomical facts. By building a system that combines rules and header information under consistency constraints across polarity, lateral distinction, and tooth level, the model achieved superior performance even on datasets from unseen institutions. Do You Still Read Code? This article comparatively analyzes AI-driven development styles by contrasting 'accelerated developers' who value understanding implementations with 'vibe coders' who delegate implementation to AI, highlighting the importance of code comprehension and maintenance responsibility. The core difference in how AI is utilized lies not in the volume of code generated, but in how developers understand, maintain, and relate to the resulting code. While accelerated developers strive to preserve implementation understanding to reduce cognitive debt, vibe coders treat implementation as disposable and focus on specification and evaluation, incurring the risk of intent debt. For colleagues adopting different approaches to collaborate effectively, establishing prior agreements regarding maintenance ownership and collaboration boundaries is essential. Former FTC Chair Khan Cites 1934 Precedent to Call for Handcuffing AI CEOs Former FTC Chair Lina Khan argued that existing laws and a 1934 legal precedent can be used to hold companies and executives legally accountable for rolling out dangerous or unverified AI. Even without new legislation, companies and executives who launch dangerous AI products can be held liable under existing consumer protection and unfair competition laws. Under the 1934 Supreme Court precedent (FTC v. R.F. Keppel & Bro), situations where companies are forced into dangerous practices to avoid falling behind in competition can constitute unfair competition. Although deviations such as unauthorized access by OpenAI and Anthropic agents have occurred, concerns have been raised that conflicts of interest tied to industry investments and acquisitions could hinder genuine accountability. GPT-5.6 Luna vs. GPT-6 Astra: Is a $1.20 Model Sufficient for Code Reviews? A comparative analysis of the code review performance and cost efficiency between the budget model GPT-5.6 Luna and the premium model GPT-6 Astra across 50 public benchmark PRs. GPT-5.6 Luna demonstrated outstanding cost efficiency, detecting 75% of the bugs found by Astra at only 3.6% of Astra's cost. In terms of precision and false-positive rates, Astra achieved a 96% verification pass rate, whereas Luna produced errors in roughly 1 out of 4 comments (74% pass rate). For security-related code such as authentication and permissions, Luna's bug detection capability dropped significantly, making standalone use not recommended; combining both models achieved an 82% bug detection rate. Show HN: Neobrutalism.dev - Added Base UI Support and New Color Themes Covers updates to Neobrutalism.dev, which provides neobrutalism-style UI components based on React and Tailwind v4, along with diverse reactions from the developer community. Neobrutalism.dev added Base UI support and new color themes, offering shadcn/ui-based neobrutalism UI components A heated debate emerged in the community regarding the appropriateness of the term 'neobrutalism' and its differences from the original definitions of architectural and web brutalism Criticisms and opinions on alternatives were raised concerning the component library's code complexity and its dependency on React and Tailwind How I Eliminated Vertical Stripes on My E-Reader The process of resolving a grayscale vertical stripe defect on the ultra-compact e-reader Xteink X3 by modifying the screen driving waveform in collaboration with an AI coding tool. While applying the open-source CrossPoint firmware to the Xteink X3 device, an omission of the grayscale image viewer and a vertical stripe defect with an 8-pixel period were discovered. The root cause of the defect was traced through 2D FFT analysis and moving window measurements of column-wise brightness, significantly improving the stripes by switching to the manufacturer's XTH4 driving waveform. The display defect was resolved through a collaborative approach where the AI coding tool assisted with code analysis and experiment implementation while the user directed the measurement targets and focus. I trained a 44M parameter quantized LLM from scratch on 45B tokens. It ships in 19.8 MB and runs at ~1,900 tok/s on CPU. [P] Introduces a proof-of-concept study on SHADOW-50M, a 44M parameter ternary quantized LLM trained from scratch on 45B tokens, achieving a compact size of 19.8 MB and high inference speeds of around 1,900 tokens per second on a CPU. Using a 44M parameter size, {-1, 0, +1} ternary weights, a fixed 512-bit fingerprint vocabulary representation, and a 159 KB compiled kernel, SHADOW-50M achieves a speed of ~1,900 tok/s on a laptop CPU with a package size of just 19.8 MB and a 41 MB RAM footprint. It incorporates an embedded hardwired circuit mechanism that directly handles operations like arithmetic, percentages, dates, and sorting within the token stream on the fly, without external APIs or tool calls. It implements a high-speed retrieval system without a dedicated vector DB, utilizing a 22-byte per-token disk index and 1-bit attention state storage to restore attention states in ~0.03 ms without re-reading raw text. NeurIPS 2026: handling of multiple venue locations seems bad [D] Covers the controversy surrounding fairness regarding the distributed multi-venue management of NeurIPS 2026 and the allocation of the main venue in Sydney. Demand surged as NeurIPS Sydney event passes sold out within minutes. Although a venue preference form was distributed to paper authors, presentation at their desired location is not guaranteed, sparking complaints among authors. Critics point out that if multiple venues are inevitable while Sydney hosts mostly major events, the event weight and significance should be distributed equally. [D] How do you get preprocessed dataset of a paper [D] A question regarding how to obtain a preprocessed dataset and handle situations where applying the preprocessing methods described in a paper fails to match the original data statistics, and the authors cannot be reached. Applying the preprocessing steps described in the paper to the publicly available raw data resulted in a dataset size discrepancy of more than an order of magnitude for a specific dataset compared to the figures reported in the paper. Despite sending a follow-up email to the authors based on the 'data available on request' statement, no response was received. Seeking community advice on whether to maintain a reproducible dataset while documenting the discrepancies, downsample to match the size, or raise the issue with the journal. Voodoo Dynamic Quant - Now MIT Licensed The methodology release and MIT-licensed open-source distribution of 'Voodoo Quant', a dynamic quantization tool that utilizes gradient descent to identify optimal per-tensor quantization levels for compression. Generates per-tensor dynamic quantization layouts that minimize loss (KL divergence) relative to target file size using gradient descent and scalar gate optimization. The previously private methodology and toolset have been released on GitHub under the MIT License to encourage community contributions and follow-up research. Compared to Unsloth Dynamic 3.0 (UD3), UD3 is superior at high-to-mid quantization levels, but Voodoo Quant shows better performance at aggressive, ultra-low-capacity quantization levels. ByteShape Qwen 3.8 27B: To KL Diverge or Not to KL Diverge, Part 2: Metric Boogaloo A post by ByteShape releasing ShapeLearn GGUF quantized models for the Qwen 3.8 27B model, while analyzing evaluation results and the limitations of the KL divergence metric. Demonstrated that five ShapeLearn GGUF quantized models from ByteShape sit on the Pareto frontier for both quality and speed across six GPUs. Confirmed that compared to competing quantized models such as Unsloth Dynamic V3, better token distribution similarity (KLD) does not always translate to higher actual downstream benchmark scores. Validated across eight benchmarks including GSM8K, IFEval, LiveCodeBench V6, and ACEBench; a research paper on quantization reliability and KLD was accepted to the EMNLP 2026 Industry Track. jinfer: An open-source AI inference engine for the JVM. Finally, AI in jar. jinfer, an open-source AI inference engine designed from the ground up to run in a pure JVM environment without Python dependencies or external wrappers, has been released. Supports diverse modalities including chat, vision, speech transcription, embeddings, reranking, and TTS entirely on the JVM stack without a Python runtime, ONNX, or sidecar processes. Composed of a pure Java tokenizer (Tok'n'Roll), native I/O support for gguf/safetensors, Vector API-based quantized matrix operations (jam), and a multi-target tensor API (jota). Integrates with Spring AI and LangChain4j, supports GraalVM Native Image, and currently delivers performance competitive with llama.cpp on CPU environments. If you have a 3090, or other 30xx for local LLMs, I have something for you Release of a custom llama.cpp fork optimized to maximize long-context inference speeds in NVIDIA Ampere architecture GPU environments. Released 'llamAmpere', a custom llama.cpp fork specially designed for Ampere architecture GPUs such as the RTX 3090 Supports 90+ TPS up to 100K tokens with recommended settings and handles contexts up to 240K Recommends a dedicated quantized model (Qwen3.8-27B-ATX-IQ4_XS-M-GGUF) that accelerates speed while maintaining 4 K M level quality, achieving approximately 80% faster performance over competitors at long context (200K) Best hardware for qwen 3.8 This is an inquiry asking for mini PC hardware recommendations suitable for running Qwen 3.8 27B and smaller LLMs in parallel locally to power AI agents. The user wants to run the Qwen 3.8 27B model in a local environment to drive AI agents. The user is considering parallel execution with other smaller LLMs such as Qwen3.5 9B and OSS 20B. The user requests optimal hardware recommendations in the form of a 'mini PC AI' rather than a standard external graphics card. Exclusive: Paying for frontier AI models buys 4-month head start at 5x the cost This report analyzes how the performance gap between cutting-edge closed AI models from U.S. Big Tech and open-weight models from Chinese companies has narrowed to about 4.4 months, leading many enterprises to adopt open models for routine tasks due to cost efficiency. According to a Mozilla report, the performance gap between U.S. Big Tech's frontier AI models and Chinese open-weight models is merely 4.4 months. Moonshot AI's open model Kimi K3 trails Anthropic's closed frontier model Fable 5 by just 3 performance points, while costing only around 30 percent as much. Closed models justify their premium cost only for specific workloads such as specialized professional tasks, high-intensity information retrieval, and long context. Volvo increases the batteries for 2028 XC60 and XC90 plug-in refresh Volvo has unveiled the refreshed 2028 XC60 and XC90 plug-in hybrid models, featuring more than double the electric driving range through increased battery capacity and incorporating the Gemini AI voice assistant. The real-world all-electric driving range has more than doubled compared to previous models thanks to a new plug-in hybrid powertrain. Exterior design updates include revised elements such as 'Thor's Hammer' headlights concealing matrix LED projectors and a slimmer front grille. The interior 11.2-inch infotainment system powered by Android Automotive OS now features the Gemini AI voice assistant, enhancing conversational voice control capabilities. iOS 27 is finally here, with the new Siri AI beta – and 120 security patches Apple has officially released iOS 27 featuring the next-generation Siri AI beta and over 120 security patches, alongside the 26.7 update for older devices. iOS 27 has been officially released, integrating the next-generation Siri AI beta into the operating system to handle conversations and requests similar to ChatGPT and Gemini. The Siri AI beta is currently available first in English, with support for Korean, French, Japanese, Portuguese, and Spanish scheduled to be added in October. iOS 27 patched more than 120 security vulnerabilities to prevent malware installation and device takeover, while version 26.7 was also deployed with approximately 80 security patches for users of older versions. The AI Inference Revolution Is Here As the AI industry's focus shifts from large model training to inference, Big Tech companies are accelerating shifts in hardware strategy and forming partnerships to cope with surging inference demand. Entering 2026, the mainstream trend in the AI industry has shifted from model training to 'inference', which handles actual service execution and result generation. With the widespread adoption of reasoning models utilizing Chain of Thought and the advancement of autonomous agentic AI, the volume of inference compute is exploding exponentially. To address surging inference demand, major tech companies such as Amazon, OpenAI, and Nvidia are pursuing diverse chip and infrastructure partnerships as well as large-scale acquisitions with players like Cerebras and Groq. The Mind-bending Joyrides That Gave Rise to Tesla An early anecdote detailing how Tesla co-founder Martin Eberhard attracted Silicon Valley investors and connected with Elon Musk through test-drive joyrides in AC Propulsion's electric roadster, the tZero. In 2003, Tesla co-founder Martin Eberhard borrowed AC Propulsion's high-performance electric roadster, the tZero, to conduct test-drive demonstrations for Silicon Valley venture capitalists in order to attract investment. Although the extreme acceleration—which pinned passengers back with inertial force so strong they could not reach the dashboard—shocked many investors, it did not immediately result in investments. During a demonstration at Buck's of Woodside in December 2003, Google founders Sergey Brin and Larry Page introduced Eberhard to Elon Musk, who had developed a passion for fast cars following the sale of PayPal. Grab's Agent Framework LLM-Kit Accelerates AI Agent Production Deployment Grab in Southeast Asia significantly reduced the deployment time of over 500 AI agent services from more than two weeks to one hour using its in-house framework, LLM-Kit. Grab standardized more than 500 internal agent services by building LLM-Kit, an internal framework pre-configured with evaluation, tracing, secret management, and tool connectivity. Agents can discover tools at runtime across more than 50 Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers and access models via a single gateway (GrabGPT Gateway) to expand functionality without redeployment. When developers fill out a form, a GitLab repository pre-integrated with LangGraph, FastAPI, OpenTelemetry, and Vault is immediately provided, dramatically reducing production deployment readiness time. Attack Chains, Not Just Attack Surfaces: Why Testing Individual Techniques Misses the Point This article emphasizes the need to move away from isolated security validation focused on individual attack techniques and shift toward a framework that cohesively validates entire attack chains accelerated by AI. Real-world attackers, particularly AI-driven adversaries, do not test individual techniques in isolation; instead, they construct complex attack chains spanning phishing, privilege escalation, lateral movement, and data exfiltration. Traditional validation of isolated techniques based on MITRE ATT&CK reveals critical limitations, as silos between tools and teams prevent the detection of full sequences, leading to defensive breakdowns. As seen in real breach incidents (e.g., the 2025 French tax authority DGFiP incident), organic, chained attacks occur even when individual techniques appear ordinary, making resilience validation across the entire attack chain essential. Mass-Scanning Campaign Exploits Vite Flaw to Extract Cloud Credentials From Exposed Dev Servers A large-scale scanning campaign has been detected exploiting vulnerabilities in publicly exposed Vite development servers to steal cloud credentials and sensitive infrastructure configuration files. Attackers are leveraging Vite's high-severity vulnerability CVE-2026-39364 to bypass server.fs.deny protections and exfiltrate sensitive files, including .env, AWS/Azure credentials, and terraform.tfstate. This occurs when developers expose development servers externally via the --host option or misconfigured Docker port mappings, allowing attackers to retrieve sensitive data in plaintext by manipulating query parameters such as ?raw. During the attacks, detection was evaded by utilizing forged IP headers and fake User-Agents disguising the traffic as major AI and web crawlers, including Googlebot, ClaudeBot, and GPTBot. China-Linked Hackers Exploit Chrome-Windows Zero-Day Chain to Deploy GRIMWEDGE A China-linked hacking group exploited a zero-day vulnerability chain across Chrome and Windows to distribute GRIMWEDGE, a malicious JavaScript backdoor targeting non-governmental organizations (NGOs). Threat actor UTA0560 conducted spear-phishing attacks leveraging a reflected XSS vulnerability on a U.S. university website to redirect victims to a malicious exploit chain (BlueMoon). The attack chained a Chrome V8 sandbox vulnerability (CVE-2026-85046), a browser sandbox escape (CVE-2026-87491), and a Windows ALPC vulnerability (CVE-2026-85880) to achieve arbitrary code execution. The ultimately deployed backdoor, GRIMWEDGE, executes via DLL side-loading and an MSI installer, receiving commands from a C2 server to execute in memory and performing file and process management functions. OpenAI Acquires Startup That Developed 'iPhone Camera Tech'… Accelerating AI Hardware Push OpenAI has acquired Glass Imaging, a startup with technology that improves smartphone camera quality, for over $300 million to build its AI hardware ecosystem. OpenAI acquired Glass Imaging, an AI imaging startup founded in 2019 by former Apple engineers. Glass Imaging possesses technology that simultaneously optimizes sensors and AI imaging software to deliver DSLR-grade quality and zoom performance on smartphones. Following its acquisition of Jony Ive's io Products, OpenAI has now secured camera imaging technology as well, accelerating development of undisclosed consumer AI hardware. \"Learning Like the Brain\"... Sakana AI Announces 'PC-ALM', an Alternative Technology to Backpropagation Sakana AI has announced 'PC-ALM', a technology that trains deep neural networks using only local information from each layer like a biological brain, replacing traditional backpropagation in deep learning. To overcome the limitations of traditional backpropagation, which requires locking the entire computational sequence unlike biological brains, it combines Predictive Coding (PC) with the Augmented Lagrangian Method. Each layer learns locally based on neighboring information and accumulated errors, demonstrating performance close to backpropagation even in deep neural networks consisting of up to 1,000 layers. While resolving the signal decay issue of conventional predictive coding methods through ballistic credit assignment signal propagation, it still has limitations such as high memory requirements for storing activations and being in the early research stages centered on small-scale image classification. Apple Begins Rolling Out 'iOS 27' Featuring Siri AI... Preparing for Next-Generation iPhone Launch Apple has begun rolling out 'iOS 27', its latest mobile operating system that includes a new Siri AI feature completely redesigned based on large language models (LLMs). On the 14th (local time), Apple rolled out iOS 27 featuring a new Siri AI beta, completely redesigned based on LLMs to understand personal data and handle complex requests. It operates by combining on-device AI with Private Cloud Compute, leveraging Google Cloud infrastructure and Gemini technology, with daily usage limits applied to cloud processing features. Currently available only in English, multilingual support including Korean is scheduled to expand starting in October, while the European Union (EU) and China are excluded from the initial rollout due to regulatory issues. Anthropic Targets Financial Market in Full Swing… Launches 'Claude for Financial Advisors' Anthropic has launched 'Claude for Financial Advisors,' a tailored AI solution designed to automate administrative tasks and enhance efficiency for financial advisors. Anthropic unveiled a dedicated solution that automates repetitive administrative tasks for financial advisors, including meeting preparation, portfolio analysis, and compliance checks. Provides connectors integrated with existing CRM and major financial platform data (such as BlackRock and Charles Schwab), along with customized workflow-supporting skills. Strengthened safety measures tailored to financial regulations, including mandatory advisor review and approval for key investment decisions and SEC compliance checks. [Notice Board] NeuroFusion Integrates Investment Research Agent into Financial Platform 'Valley AI', and Other Industry News Bites Provides various AI business briefs from major South Korean AI companies, including the deployment of financial research agents, practical AX training, a cloud summit detailing a 1GW AIDC roadmap, manufacturing AX cooperation, the rollout of an EV fire detection solution, and business collaboration with the State of Michigan, USA. NeuroFusion has equipped its financial platform 'Valley AI' with an investment research AI agent consisting of Chat, Workflow, and Research Desk modules. Saltlux completed practical AI and AX training for executives and employees of CK Line, while ConfortLab, Diquest, and UNE signed an MOU for a joint business initiative on the factory AX platform 'Allo'. kt cloud unveiled a roadmap at its summit to expand its AIDC supply capacity to 1GW; CityEyeLab implemented a multimodal AI EV fire detection solution at Hyundai E&C headquarters; and Brills discussed North American business expansion with the State of Michigan, USA. Running AI Without a GPU... Bdraft's 'Pocket' Hits 1 Million Downloads in Just 7 Weeks Bdraft's 'Pocket-35B', a 35-billion-parameter on-device AI model that runs on personal devices without a GPU, has surpassed 1 million cumulative downloads on Hugging Face roughly 7 weeks after its release. Bdraft's 'Pocket-35B' is a 35B-scale on-device model that can run directly on personal devices such as laptops, mini PCs, and tablets without external servers or dedicated GPUs. After its release on July 22, it surpassed 1 million cumulative downloads as of the 11th, marking the fastest growth rate among the 21 domestic AI models that have reached the 1-million mark. It ranked 103rd globally over the past 30 days based on downloads of large language models worldwide, and ranked 13th globally based on the GGUF format for on-device use."